		"nextNodeId": 109,
		"nodes": {
			"19": {
				"attributes": {
					"alignment": {
						"type": "integer",
						"value": 0
					},
					"fill": {
						"type": "bool",
						"value": true
					},
					"font-size": {
						"type": "float",
						"value": 19
					},
					"text": {
						"type": "string",
						"value": "COLOR\n\nColor is one of the most important aspects of your visuals.\nIn this tutorial we'll deep dive into how color works in Wire.\nYou will learn how to create colors, how to create color variants and how to make color variations."
					}
				},
				"bounds": {
					"height": 116,
					"width": 671,
					"x": 664,
					"y": 335.5
				},
				"class": {
					"id": "77697265-3C1D-467D-A722-0FC566538374",
					"version": 1
				},
				"clock": "video",
				"color": "ffd0c117",
				"constants": {},
				"hidden": [
					"text",
					"alignment",
					"font-size",
					"fill"
				],
				"name": "Comment",
				"thumbnail_visible": true
			},
			"20": {
				"attributes": {
					"alignment": {
						"type": "integer",
						"value": 0
					},
					"fill": {
						"type": "bool",
						"value": false
					},
					"font-size": {
						"type": "float",
						"value": 19
					},
					"text": {
						"type": "string",
						"value": "RGBA\n\nColors in wire consist of four channels: red, green, blue and alpha.\nRed, Green and Blue produce the color.\nAlpha determines the opacity of the color.\n\nThe color values in Wire range from 0 to 1.\nA value of 0 in a channel represents the total absence of that channel.\nA value of 1 in a channel means that it is turned up to the max.\n\nThe RGBA system is an additive color system.\nThis means that R+G+B+A produces the final color.\n\nIn Wire an RGBA color is represented with the Color type.\nWhen we max out every channel [1.1.1.1] we get a white color.\nWhen we zero everything except alpha [0.0.0.1] we get solid black.\n"
					}
				},
				"bounds": {
					"height": 729,
					"width": 758.00750732421875,
					"x": 664,
					"y": 479.5
				},
				"class": {
					"id": "77697265-3C1D-467D-A722-0FC566538374",
					"version": 1
				},
				"clock": "video",
				"color": "ffff6a00",
				"constants": {},
				"hidden": [
					"text",
					"alignment",
					"font-size",
					"fill"
				],
				"name": "Comment",
				"thumbnail_visible": true
			},
			"29": {
				"attributes": {
					"alignment": {
						"type": "integer",
						"value": 0
					},
					"fill": {
						"type": "bool",
						"value": false
					},
					"font-size": {
						"type": "float",
						"value": 19
					},
					"text": {
						"type": "string",
						"value": "COLOR NODES\n\nThere are three important color nodes that you should know about: Color, Color In and Gradient Palette.\n\nThe Color node will give you a color picker to create a color.\nThe color picker will also suggest complementary colors to the one selected, making it easier for you to produce striking palettes. This node can also be manually instantiated to create palettes using the multi-editor (CMD\/Control + Click a value box to open up the multi-editor on an instantiated node. \n\nThe Color In node is the same as the color node, but in an interactive format.\nThis node will appear on the Dashboard panel and therefore in the Arena\/Avenue interface.\nThe Color In node cannot be instantiated.\n\nThe Linear node can produce a collection of colors based on the two input colors.\nIt will generate a linear gradient between the two colors.\n"
					}
				},
				"bounds": {
					"height": 742.07708740234375,
					"width": 716.7093505859375,
					"x": 664.5382080078125,
