			"40": {
				"attributes": {
					"alignment": {
						"type": "integer",
						"value": 0
					},
					"fill": {
						"type": "bool",
						"value": false
					},
					"font-size": {
						"type": "float",
						"value": 19
					},
					"text": {
						"type": "string",
						"value": "APPLYING COLOR\n\nBy now you know how to apply colors to shapes.\nBut there are also methods to apply colors to existing textures.\nThe most notable nodes for this are Colorize and Tint.\n\nColorize will colorize the input texture to a given color.\nIt also has a palette mode which colorizes the input texture to the palette texture.\n\nThe Tint node will colorize a texture with two colors depending on the greyscale value.\n"
					}
				},
				"bounds": {
					"height": 918.6126708984375,
					"width": 1554.359375,
					"x": 664,
					"y": 1235.5
				},
				"class": {
					"id": "77697265-3C1D-467D-A722-0FC566538374",
					"version": 1
				},
				"clock": "video",
				"color": "fff26eb5",
				"constants": {},
				"hidden": [
					"text",
					"alignment",
					"font-size",
					"fill"
				],
				"name": "Comment",
				"thumbnail_visible": true
			},
			"41": {
				"attributes": {
					"alignment": {
						"type": "integer",
						"value": 0
					},
					"fill": {
						"type": "bool",
						"value": false
					},
					"font-size": {
						"type": "float",
						"value": 19
					},
					"text": {
						"type": "string",
						"value": "RANDOM COLOR & FLOAT 4\n\nRandom colors can be generated by the Random node by setting the Random node to Float4.\nWire will convert the Float4 type automatically to a Color type for you using the Float 4's elements for the RGBA channels.\n\nIn this example we've set the Alpha range to a min and max of 1 to make sure we don't get any transparency.\n\nFor this example we've used a Random node, but you could pack any values you like into a Float4 node and run with it. You could use this method to create colors out of oscillators, user input or even the current date and time!"
					}
				},
